  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered afternoon/evening showers/storms. \nSummertime pattern continues into early next week then a cool\ndown.\n\n\nAt the surface, a cold front will move southward into the Mid-\nAtlantic today. Ahead of the front, a moist airmass will be in \nplace over much of the area . Surface dewpoints within this \nairmass will be in the 60s to near 70 F. As surface temperatures\nwarm today, an axis of moderate instability will develop along \nthe moist axis. In response to surface heating, scattered \nthunderstorms will form in the mountains and move southward \ninto the Piedmont and foothills. The thermodynamic environment \nmay be enough to support an isolated wind-damage threat, mainly \nin the late afternoon and early evening when low- level lapse \nrates become steepest, especially along/south of the VA/NC\nborder.\n\nThe front shift south Saturday but close enough to keep\nshower/storm chances around with higher probabilities over the\nsouthern Blue Ridge, and less across the central VA Piedmont.\nAirmass should be a little more stable so not expecting anything\nsevere.\n\nThe front works north Sunday as a warm front, then drops again\nearly next week, while upper trough starts to dig southeast into\nthe Ohio Valley. Should see an uptick in rain chances Monday-\nTuesday. By mid to late week the fronts should be east and \nsouth of us and high pressure arrives from the north. However, \nthe upper trough remains northwest of the area and will keep \nshower chances around while temperatures cool to below normal. \nFor most, this will be much needed rain, but still overall \ndoesn't look like a complete washout. Just keep the umbrellas \nhandy at times.",
